Abyssal Shunt
Ingredients: CHICAGO / CITY / URBAN BLUES + TECH HOUSE + HYPNOTIC, TRANCE-LIKE & MINIMAL + Marimba
Creative Catalyst: The sound was discovered by deep-sea divers repairing transatlantic data cables.

Sonic Description
It sounds like a rusted-out South Side juke joint submerged four miles down at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, where the heavy, weeping bend of a bottleneck slide guitar dissolves into the relentless, four-on-the-floor pulse of Berlin minimal techno. The sonic space is claustrophobic yet infinite, anchored by water-logged acoustic marimbas striking with the heavy thud of anchor chains. Microtonal blues quarter-tones glitch against the hypnotic loops of deep-sea pressure compressors, creating an oppressive, trance-inducing undertow that feels as though it is being pumped directly into your inner ear through a pressurized diving helmet.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 122 to 126 BPM, operating on a hypnotic, locked-groove tech house skeletal kick, punctuated by rubbery, syncopated Chicago blues shuffle micro-timing.
- Harmonic Palette: Minor pentatonic scales warped by sub-audible hydro-acoustic detuning, resulting in eerie quarter-tone micro-bends over icy minor-ninth tech house chord pads.
- Production Signatures: Hydrophone-captured metallic cable vibrations used as sidechained percussion, compressed vacuum-tube blues saturation drowned in algorithmic convolution reverb, and granularly stretched marimba transients acting as rhythmic oscillators.
- Dynamics: A glacial, subterranean build where tracks never truly drop, but rather increase in water pressure—shifting from sparse, bone-dry low-end pulses to a crushing, saturated wall of brine-soaked rhythm.

Scene & Origin Lore
Born in 2029 inside the high-pressure saturation chambers of commercial salvage ships docked off the coast of Halifax, Abyssal Shunt is the guarded secret of saturation divers and deep-sea splicers. Wearing reinforced silica-gel wetsuits caked in dried seabed salt and rust, these workers spend weeks breathing trimix gas, listening to the agonizing groans of transatlantic fiber-optic bundles stretching across the ocean floor. When they return to surface, they smuggle home waterproof flash drives containing the raw, unfiltered hydrophone recordings of the cables—sounds they manipulate using modular gear modified to withstand three hundred atmospheres. Their clandestine parties, known as Sump-Locks, take place in decommissioned ballast tanks where attendees wear modified diving helms filled with low-frequency tactile transducers, swaying in slow, synchronized unison to the heavy, oceanic drag of the beat.

Central Instruments
- Hydraulophone Marimba: A custom percussion instrument built from salvaged marine-grade titanium bars, played underwater with heavy rubber mallets inside a pressurized acoustic chamber.
- Resonator Bottle-Slide Guitars: Hollow-body electric guitars fitted with heavy brass tubes, wired directly into stolen cable-repair amplifiers to capture the literal electromagnetic hum of the seabed.
- Sub-Sea Step Sequencers: Modified analog triggers synced to the heartbeat and diving-bell pressure meters of deep-sea workers.

